component SigilAgentCapabilityContract {
  goal {
    Own the versioned provider-neutral capability and observability declarations
    shared by compiler profiles, evaluation skills, and CLI adapters.

    Make deterministic compatibility matching possible without claiming that
    self-reported adapter behavior is independently verified.
  }

  interface {
    AgentCapabilityContract {
      schemaVersion is 1 and its closed capability dimensions are workspaceAccess of
      none, read-only, or read-write; agentToolNetwork and approvalEscalation
      booleans; and statePersistence of ephemeral or persistent

      compiler evaluation requests require read-only workspace access, no agent-tool
      network, and no approval escalation; each evaluator request resolves state
      persistence from the exactly selected adapter declaration

      an adapter declaration matches only when schemaVersion is 1, all four
      dimensions are present, and every value exactly equals its requested value

      an omitted dimension, unknown value, unknown schema version, or undeclared
      capability dimension rejects matching; implementation identity, version,
      provider metadata, and observability metadata do not participate in capability
      equality

      declarations are self-reported compatibility metadata rather than verified
      safety guarantees
    }

    AdapterObservabilityDeclaration {
      progress describes a live observation channel and is none; usage and cost are
      independently unavailable, partial, or final; tokenBudgetEnforcement and
      costBudgetEnforcement are each unavailable, preflight, live, or
      post-settlement-only

      only preflight and live budget enforcement are preventive; progress none
      prohibits live progress emissions but permits optional progress observations
      inside validated terminal result metadata; progress always resolves to none,
      while omitted or unknown usage, cost, tokenBudgetEnforcement, and
      costBudgetEnforcement resolve to unavailable
    }
  }
}

expand SigilAgentCapabilityContract {
  decisions {
    ClosedVersionedCapabilityVocabulary {
      Decision: Use one versioned closed capability schema with exact matching for
      workspace access, agent tool network access, approval escalation, and state
      persistence.

      Scope: Governs capability requests and self-declarations for every supported
      adapter.

      Trade-offs: Capability extensions require a schema-version and compiler
      contract change.

      Design issues addressed: Unversioned open-ended declarations let plugins
      interpret omitted, unknown, or extra dimensions inconsistently.

      Consequences: Equivalent declarations produce the same match result without
      treating unrelated adapter metadata as a capability.
    }

    SelfDeclaredAdapterCapabilities {
      Decision: Treat adapter capability and observability declarations as
      self-reported compatibility metadata, with explicit adapter selection accepting
      the documented implementation risk.

      Scope: Governs compiler-owned and plugin-supplied adapter declarations.

      Trade-offs: Matching declarations do not guarantee filesystem, network,
      command, persistence, or data-handling restrictions.

      Discarded alternatives: Inferring enforcement from provider events and
      certifying plugin behavior were rejected as unreliable.

      Consequences: Trust limitations remain documentation-only and do not enter
      logs, events, reports, diagnostics, status, or evaluator prompts.
    }

    EvaluatorPersistenceRequirement {
      Decision: Resolve each evaluation request state-persistence requirement from
      the exactly selected adapter declaration while retaining fixed read-only,
      offline, and approval-free requirements.

      Scope: Governs compiler evaluation requests and adapter capability matching;
      excludes claims that a self-declared capability is independently enforced.

      Trade-offs: Evaluators without ephemeral execution remain usable and may leave
      provider-managed state, while exact implementation binding keeps the accepted
      declaration reproducible.

      Design issues addressed: Requiring ephemeral persistence for every evaluator
      makes OpenCode unavailable even though its CLI can satisfy the other inspection
      restrictions and exposes no ephemeral option.

      Discarded alternatives: Declaring OpenCode ephemeral and rejecting every
      persistent adapter were rejected because the former is false and the latter
      prevents explicitly selected persistent evaluators.

      Consequences: An adapter requests the state persistence it can honor; each
      remains subject to exact capability matching and documented trust limitations.

      Revisit when: OpenCode exposes an enforceable ephemeral execution mode or
      another supported adapter changes its persistence option.
    }

    IndependentObservabilityCapabilities {
      Decision: Model terminal usage, cost, and token or cost budget enforcement as
      independent declared observability capabilities while live progress is none.

      Scope: Governs all supported CLI adapters.

      Trade-offs: Some evaluations cannot report progress or conclusive usage, cost,
      or budget outcomes.

      Consequences: Terminal-only providers remain usable without invented events or
      telemetry; compiler-owned stage progress remains independent.
    }
  }

  cases {
    AgentCapabilityContract {
      A complete schema version 1 declaration exactly matching all four requested
      values satisfies compatibility without proving enforcement.

      An omitted capability dimension or unknown schema version mismatches before
      provider invocation.

      An implementation version and progress declaration do not affect capability
      equality.

      Omitted or unknown progress resolves to none for canonical observability
      comparison; omitted or unknown remaining observability fields resolve to
      unavailable.

      An adapter declaring progress none emits no live progress observations but may
      include provider-observed progress metadata in its one validated terminal
      result without changing canonical observability equality.

      An evaluator declaring ephemeral persistence receives an ephemeral request,
      while one declaring persistent state receives a persistent request without
      weakening the other capability dimensions.
    }
  }
}
